    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Kaifeng began as the Wei capital Daliang in 361 BCE, was destroyed when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> conquered Wei, and revived as a canal town, Junyi, Bianzhou and then the capital of four of the Five Dynasties.</li><li>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>, which made it its capital in 960, Dongjing became the largest and most prosperous city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and it is believed to have been the largest city in the world from 1013 to 1127.</li><li>Jin forces took it in 1127 and Mongol forces in 1233, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> flooded it repeatedly, most destructively in 1642, after which only 30,000 of its 370,000 people remained.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The astronomical clock tower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/su-song">Su Song</a> at Dongjing was crowned with a water-powered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armillary-sphere">armillary sphere</a> and incorporated an escapement mechanism two hundred years before escapements were found in the clockworks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

