      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">361 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">King Hui of Wei builds the city of Daliang</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">202</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> repairs the Suiyang Canal at Junyi, opening the Bian River to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> and Huaihe</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">781</span><span class="rf-text">Li Mian builds a new city wall at Bianzhou</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">960</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> makes the city its capital</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1127</span><span class="rf-text">Jin forces occupy Dongjing and take the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> imperial family north</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1233</span><span class="rf-text">The city falls to Mongol forces after a siege of ten months</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1642</span><span class="rf-text">The Ming army floods the city with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> to drown besieging rebels</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1954</span><span class="rf-text">The capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> province moves to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhengzhou">Zhengzhou</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kaifeng lies on the southern bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> in east-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spring-and-autumn-period">Spring and Autumn period</a> two ancient cities stood in its vicinity: Yi, built by the state of Wey, and Qifeng, built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-zhuang-of-zheng">Duke Zhuang of Zheng</a> south of the present-day city. Wey was later annexed by the state of Wei.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a> the lords of Wei held the title of King of Liang. In 361 BCE King Hui of Wei moved the Wei capital from the city of An to Yi and built the city of Daliang there, after which the state of Wei was called Liang. The first of many canals in the area, linking a local river to the Yellow River, was built in this period. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> conquered Wei, Daliang was destroyed and abandoned except for a mid-sized market town, which was renamed Junyi.</p><p>Qifeng was renamed Kaifeng after the Qin conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Its original name was Qifeng, and the syllable qi was changed to the essentially synonymous kai to avoid the naming taboo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-jing-of-han">Liu Qi</a>, Emperor Jing of Han. The old city of Qifeng later declined and was abandoned, and its ruins lie near the present-day town of Zhuxian.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> Junyi was the capital of the Kingdom of Liang, ruled by Liu Wu, a son of the Han emperor Wen, as Prince of Liang. Liu Wu restored the old city walls and put up many buildings, and Daliang became a centre of music and art, a refuge for artists and a city of splendid gardens, although it was of little political importance at this period. In 202 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> repaired the Suiyang Canal at Junyi, opening the Bian River to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> and Huaihe rivers. The canal allowed grain to be carried north from those regions, and Junyi, at a junction of waterway and land routes, recovered economically.</p><p>Early in the 7th century the old Daliang, now called Bianzhou, became a major commercial hub when it was connected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a> and to a canal running to western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>. In 955 Emperor Shizong of Zhou mobilised 100,000 civilians to build an outer city.</p><p>Dongjing reached its peak in the 11th century as a commercial and industrial centre at the intersection of four major canals. It was then surrounded by three rings of walls and probably had a population of between 600,000 and 700,000. It is believed to have been the largest city in the world from 1013 to 1127. Another figure puts the population at over 400,000, living both inside and outside the wall.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The city was the capital of the Wei state from 361 BCE, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> conquered Wei. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> it was the capital of the Kingdom of Liang. In the Eastern Han the kingdom became Liang County, and part of it became the Chenliu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">Commandery</a>, which was re-established as a principality under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a> and the Jin dynasty. In 534 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-xiaojing-of-eastern-wei">Emperor Xiaojing of Eastern Wei</a> changed Chenliu County to Liangzhou, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a> it was renamed Bianzhou after the Bian River. In 781, under the Tang, its governor Li Mian built a new city wall 22 li in circumference.</p><p>In the Five Dynasties period the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-liang">Later Liang</a> emperor made Bianzhou his capital, calling it Dongdu, the Eastern Capital. It was the capital of the Later Liang (913 to 923), Later Jin (936 to 946), Later Han (947 to 950) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-zhou">Later Zhou</a> (951 to 960). The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> made Bian its capital when it overthrew the Later Zhou in 960, and named it the Eastern Capital, Dongjing.</p><p>After 1127 it became in 1130 the capital of a Han-ruled puppet kingdom called Great Qi, until 1138, and then came under the direct rule of the Jin. The Jin moved their court there in 1214. Under Mongol rule it was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> Jiangbei Province, established in 1268. In 1368 the Ming captured it and restored the name Kaifeng. It remained the capital of Henan from the Ming into the early years of the People's Republic.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Dongjing Menghualu, a nostalgic memoir of the city, describes the market outside Donghua Gate, where food, seasonal fruits and flowers, fish, jade and clothing were sold, and a customer who asked for a dozen dishes to go with wine would be served at once. Besides the day markets there were night markets and dawn markets. The main street was called the Imperial Street. Patrol stations stood every two or three hundred steps, and the patrolmen directed pedestrians and vehicles by day and guarded government offices and merchants' residences at night.</p><p>The city's restaurants, taverns and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a> could entertain thousands of people, with daily performances of storytelling, singing, puppet shows, dance and acrobatics. Zhang Zeduan's painting Along the River During the Qingming Festival shows the Bian River and its banks during the festival. The chrysanthemum was grown in Kaifeng for 1,600 years, and its cultivation reached its height under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchens</a> took the city.</p><p>Under the Song the capital's government was the Kaifeng Prefecture, the only prefecture of the Capital Region. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/su-song">Su Song</a>, an engineer, scientist and statesman, built a well-known astronomical clock tower, crowned with a rotating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armillary-sphere">armillary sphere</a> driven by water. From the 8th or 9th century until the 19th, Kaifeng had the oldest surviving Jewish community in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, the Kaifeng <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. The city also has a significant Muslim enclave and a number of women's mosques, the oldest of which, the Wangjia Hutong Women's Mosque, dates to 1820.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-gaozong-of-song"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-gaozong-of-song.jpg" alt="Emperor Gaozong of Song" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-gaozong-of-song">Emperor Gaozong of Song</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Song_Gaozong.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">anonymous, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The city declined in the turmoil at the end of the Western Jin and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-kingdoms">Sixteen Kingdoms</a> era. In 1127 it was occupied by the Jin. The Jin ruler ordered all its assets and its captives, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> imperial family and officials who refused to escape, to be taken to the Jin capital, Shangjing, near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harbin">Harbin</a>. Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-gaozong-of-song">Zhao Gou</a> escaped and moved the remaining Song court away. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchens</a> withdrew during the year, but the Song court never returned, and they took the city again in 1130.</p><p>The Jin moved their own court to Kaifeng in 1214, relying on the line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>. In 1233 the city fell to Mongol forces after a siege of ten months, and it was looted and all the Jin family members were killed. In 1358 and 1359 Red Turban rebels led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-futong">Liu Futong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-liner">Han Lin'er</a> briefly held it, until they were crushed by Yuan forces.</p><p>The city lay on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a> but was militarily vulnerable on the floodplain of the Yellow River. Five major floods devastated it between 1375 and 1416, and there were more than 300 floods from the Southern Song to the late Qing. In 1410 a flood destroyed the city and killed 14,000 households. In 1642 rebels attacked Kaifeng three times, and in the third siege the Ming army flooded the city with the Yellow River to drown them. The whole city was submerged, and only 30,000 of its 370,000 people remained. It was abandoned, rebuilt in 1662, and rebuilt again in 1843 after the flood of 1841.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kaifeng is one of the Eight Ancient Capitals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, having been the capital eight times. The Iron Pagoda of the Youguo Temple, built in 1049 and 54.7 m high, survived war and floods and is the oldest landmark in the city. The Po Pagoda, dating from 974, was partly destroyed. The Daxiangguo Temple was built in 555.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> arrived at Kaifeng in 1260 and wrote about it in his diaries. From June 1938 to August 1945 the Japanese Imperial Army occupied it. In 1948 the Battle of Kaifeng between Communist and Nationalist forces ended in a victory for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> and the collapse of the Nationalist forces there. In October 1954 the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> was moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhengzhou">Zhengzhou</a>, and in September 1983 Kaifeng City was formed from former prefectures and districts.</p><p>Kaifeng cuisine plays a dominant part in forming Henan cuisine, and its night markets continue those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>. The chrysanthemum is the city flower. In 2020 the prefecture had 4,824,016 inhabitants, of whom 1,735,581 lived in the metropolitan area of five districts. It is a prefecture-level city of Henan province, with a campus of Henan University.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Kaifeng, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>, began as the Wei capital Daliang in 361 BCE. As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> capital Dongjing it was probably the largest city in the world from 1013 to 1127, and it was repeatedly taken and flooded, most destructively in 1642.</em></p>

